in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @tool
in @io

# @tool/gitlab_ci — the FIRST-order tech tool species wrapping the
# GitLab CI toolchain (GitLab 2015+; the CI-config-validator +
# CI-run-dispatcher binary wrapper, primarily via the `glab` CLI
# and/or GitLab REST API).
#
# Species under @tool at the family-root altitude landed this tick
# in `shards/tool.mirror` per Mara closure canonical spec
# `docs/specs/2026-07-18-the-compiler-in-one-sentence.md` §3.4 +
# `docs/specs/2026-07-18-stagefreight-delivery.md` §3.3 (Mara this
# tick; StageFreight Delivery Arc 1 Tick 4).
#
# === Naming convention note ===
#
# Filename uses underscore (`gitlab_ci.mirror`) per substrate-decl
# path-namespace convention. `tool_id` variant tag uses underscore
# (`gitlab_ci`). Prism family path spells `@tool/gitlab_ci`. In
# prose consumers may write `@tool/gitlab-ci` (hyphenated to match
# upstream product name); substrate reads the underscored form
# canonically per `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_namespace`.
#
# === What @tool/gitlab_ci IS ===
#
# The IDENTITY + VERSION + CONTRACT + PROVENANCE wrapper for
# gitlab-ci-adjacent invocations at TWO surfaces:
#
#   1. The `glab ci` CLI binary (upstream GitLab go-CLI) — porcelain
#      surface for lint + pipeline management + MR interaction.
#   2. The GitLab REST API — direct HTTP dispatch for CI operations
#      when the glab CLI is not pinned or when programmatic access
#      needs richer response bodies than the CLI surfaces.
#
# The species-body multiplexes per binary availability: prefer
# `glab` when present; fall through to REST API via @io/http when
# glab is absent.
#
# Composition direction (dual dispatch — StageFreight spec §3.3):
#
#   @tool/gitlab_ci(lint, [.gitlab-ci.yml])
#     ─specializes─▶ @tool.exec(tool_invocation{tool_id: gitlab_ci, ...})
#       ─dispatches (a) glab pinned─▶ @io.exec("glab", ["ci", "lint", ...])
#       ─dispatches (b) REST fallback─▶ @io/http.post(gitlab_api, {yaml: <...>})
#         ─crosses─▶ glab binary process spawn OR HTTPS request
#
# === Substrate-already-had-the-word ===
#
# Grep verdict clean:
#   - `gitlab-ci` / `gitlab_ci` word does not appear at family-root
#     altitude in existing shards.
#   - `@tool/gitlab_ci` DOES NOT EXIST prior to this tick.
#   - `@io/git/forge/gitlab` FORWARD-PROMISED (per @io/git §6
#     forge-adapter forward-promise); DIFFERENT altitude — the
#     git-forge adapter handles PR/issue/release surfaces via git
#     protocol, whereas THIS species handles CI-config validation
#     + CI-run dispatch via REST+YAML altitude.
#
# Per StageFreight spec §8 composition surprise #2: @tool/gitlab_ci
# is ORTHOGONAL to @io/git/forge/gitlab. Two GitLab-adjacent species
# emerge — git-forge adapter (git protocol; forward-promised) vs
# ci-validator + ci-dispatcher (REST+YAML; this tick). Different
# mechanisms; different altitudes; both needed.
#
# === REST+YAML altitude — orthogonal to @tool/git ===
#
# @tool/git dispatches through the git protocol (SSH / HTTPS git
# transport). @tool/gitlab_ci dispatches through GitLab's REST API
# (HTTPS JSON) + YAML validation. These are orthogonal transports:
# an invocation may compose BOTH (push commits via @tool/git.
# push_signed, then trigger pipeline via @tool/gitlab_ci.pipeline_
# create) but neither IS the other.
#
# === Substrate authority ===
#
# - Family-root: `shards/tool.mirror` (this session).
# - Canonical spec (species): `docs/specs/2026-07-18-stagefreight-
#   delivery.md` §3.3 (Mara this tick; shape substrate-decl'd there
#   and lifted to substrate here verbatim).
# - Canonical spec (family): `docs/specs/2026-07-18-the-compiler-in-
#   one-sentence.md` §3.4 (Mara `2dd8ddb`).
# - Orthogonal sibling forward-promises: `shards/io/git/forge/
#   gitlab.mirror` (git-protocol forge adapter; NOT this tick).
# - Mechanism-fallthrough sibling: `shards/io/http.mirror` (forward-
#   promised @io/http species; REST fallback path composes here).
# - Recognition #55 (form/process partition): @tool/gitlab_ci is
#   process-side (the invocation of glab or REST endpoint); the
#   `.gitlab-ci.yml` form-side lives at future `@code/gitlab_ci`
#   or `@code/yaml` refinement (forward-promised).
# - Recognition #98 candidate (content-addressing across scopes):
#   pipeline_id + job_trace hashes compose with the existing four
#   witnesses (git OID, nix derivation, cargo Cargo.lock, OCI
#   manifest, Go go.sum) as sixth altitude of content-addressing.

prism @tool/gitlab_ci {
  focus tool_invocation
  project tool_invocation
  split tool_invocation
  shift tool_invocation
  settle tool_invocation
}

# ============================================================
# §2 The carriers
# ============================================================

# === The gitlab_ci_subcommand carrier ===
#
# Closed variant naming the gitlab-ci subcommand set the substrate
# admits at the porcelain altitude (`glab ci ...` + `glab mr ...`
# surface). Extensible via mint; the FLOOR set below covers the
# subcommands StageFreight delivery + PR verification actually
# invoke.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the underlying tag.
type gitlab_ci_subcommand =
  | lint                 # gitlab-ci-lint .gitlab-ci.yml (or glab ci lint)
  | pipeline_create      # trigger pipeline via API
  | pipeline_status      # query pipeline status
  | job_trace            # fetch job trace/logs
  | artifact_download    # download job artifacts
  | mr_create            # create merge request (via glab CLI)
  | mr_status            # query MR status
  | mr_diff              # fetch MR diff

# === The gitlab_ci_pipeline_config carrier ===
#
# Species-decl'd descriptor for a GitLab CI pipeline configuration.
# Composition-primitive naming per
# `feedback_composition_primitive_naming_convention` (Alex
# 2026-07-18): the config IS a composition-primitive over the
# (yaml_path, project_id, include_refs, variables) input-shape.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the (yaml_path, project_id,
# include_refs, variables) quadruple.
type gitlab_ci_pipeline_config = {
  yaml_path: ref,            # e.g. "./.gitlab-ci.yml"
  project_id: ref,           # e.g. "12345" or "gitlab.com/foo/proj"
  include_refs: ref,         # e.g. ["main", "develop"]
  variables: ref,            # map of CI variable name → value
}

# === The gitlab_ci_pipeline_handle carrier ===
#
# Typed handle for a triggered pipeline — the (project_id,
# pipeline_id, ref, sha) tuple that gitlab API responses carry.
# Composes with @tool/gitlab_ci.pipeline_status for status-poll
# operations.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the (project_id, pipeline_id,
# ref, sha) quadruple.
type gitlab_ci_pipeline_handle = {
  project_id: ref,
  pipeline_id: ref,
  ref: ref,              # git ref that triggered the pipeline
  sha: ref,              # git sha at trigger
}

# === The gitlab_ci_pipeline_status carrier ===
#
# Closed variant enumerating the terminal + transient states GitLab
# CI pipelines occupy. Load-bearing for the
# `gitlab_ci_pipeline_terminates` bilateral gate.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the underlying tag.
type gitlab_ci_pipeline_status =
  | pending
  | running
  | success             # terminal
  | failed              # terminal
  | canceled            # terminal
  | skipped             # terminal
  | manual              # transient
  | scheduled           # transient

# ============================================================
# §3 The actions
# ============================================================

# === exec action — specialization of @tool.exec ===
#
# Dispatch a gitlab_ci subcommand invocation. Body multiplexes:
#
#   - `glab` binary present + pinned  ─▶ @io.exec("glab", ...)
#   - `glab` absent OR REST preferred ─▶ @io/http.post/get/put(gitlab_api, ...)
#
# The multiplex decision is made per-invocation at dispatch time;
# species-body composes @tool/nix.resolve_pin for glab presence
# check.
exec(subcommand: gitlab_ci_subcommand, args: ref, invocation: tool_invocation)
  -> tool { \ }

# === lint_config action — YAML-schema-validation load-bearing dispatch ===
#
# Specialization of exec for CI YAML lint. THE gate action for
# StageFreight PR delivery per spec §4.3 (@roomba emits `@tool
# (gitlab_ci, [lint, .gitlab-ci.yml])` in the invocation sequence).
#
# Discharges via `glab ci lint --config <path>` OR REST API
# `POST /api/v4/ci/lint` per the multiplex.
#
# Splinter-pole: YAML parses; schema validates; no schema drift
# diagnostics; exit_code / API response indicates VALID.
# Narcissus-pole: YAML parse error; schema violation diagnostics;
# deprecated syntax warnings surface as candidate bugfixes per
# spec §6.5 GitLab CI schema drift.
lint_config(config: gitlab_ci_pipeline_config, invocation: tool_invocation)
  -> verdict { \ }

# === pipeline_create action ===
#
# Trigger a new pipeline against `(project_id, ref, variables)`.
# Discharges via `glab ci run` OR REST API `POST /api/v4/projects/
# :id/pipeline` per multiplex. Returns a gitlab_ci_pipeline_handle.
pipeline_create(config: gitlab_ci_pipeline_config, invocation: tool_invocation)
  -> gitlab_ci_pipeline_handle { \ }

# === pipeline_status action ===
#
# Query the current status of a pipeline handle. Discharges via
# `glab ci status` OR REST API `GET /api/v4/projects/:id/pipelines/
# :pipeline_id` per multiplex. Returns gitlab_ci_pipeline_status.
pipeline_status(handle: gitlab_ci_pipeline_handle, invocation: tool_invocation)
  -> gitlab_ci_pipeline_status { \ }

# === version_of action ===
#
# Resolve the pinned glab CLI version. Reads tools{} block pin OR
# discharges through @io/nix (sibling @tool/nix species) OR
# through system PATH. For the REST-fallback path, version_of
# reads the GitLab-Version response header from `GET /api/v4/
# metadata`.
version_of(pin: ref) -> ref { \ }

# ============================================================
# §4 The bilaterals
# ============================================================

# === gitlab_ci_subcommand_admissible sub-bilateral ===
#
# Bilateral verdict that a gitlab_ci_subcommand + args combination
# is admissible per glab CLI's own grammar OR REST API's schema.
#
# Splinter-pole: subcommand parses; args are valid for that
# subcommand under the active multiplex (CLI vs REST).
# Narcissus-pole: unknown subcommand; args violate schema.
gitlab_ci_subcommand_admissible(subcommand: gitlab_ci_subcommand, args: ref,
                                p: perturbation) -> verdict { \ }

# === gitlab_ci_config_valid composed bilateral ===
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING bilateral for StageFreight PR verification.
# Commitment that the `.gitlab-ci.yml` file parses AND validates
# per GitLab's active-instance schema.
#
# Splinter-pole: YAML parses cleanly; schema validation passes;
# no deprecated syntax warnings; all `include` / `extends` /
# `!reference` chains resolve; job graph is well-formed.
# Narcissus-pole: YAML parse error; schema drift diagnostics
# (deprecated `only:` / `except:` when GitLab has moved to
# `rules:`; missing required fields); unresolved `include` refs;
# job graph cycles.
#
# Per StageFreight spec §6.5: schema drift IS a candidate bugfix
# mirror surfaces in Marcus's StageFreight repo via THIS bilateral.
bilateral gitlab_ci_config_valid {
  sentinel "tool=gitlab-ci-yaml-parses-and-validates-per-schema"
  arity 2
}
gitlab_ci_config_valid(invocation: tool_invocation, p: perturbation)
  -> verdict { \ }

# === gitlab_ci_pipeline_terminates composed bilateral ===
#
# Bilateral commitment that a triggered pipeline reaches a terminal
# state (success / failed / canceled / skipped) within a bounded
# time budget — does NOT hang indefinitely in pending / running /
# manual / scheduled states.
#
# Splinter-pole: pipeline reaches terminal state within budget;
# terminal state captured via pipeline_status polling; witness
# signed via @trust chain.
# Narcissus-pole: pipeline stuck in transient state past budget;
# manual gate not triggered; scheduled state not resolved;
# infinite-poll condition detected.
bilateral gitlab_ci_pipeline_terminates {
  sentinel "tool=pipeline-reaches-terminal-state-not-stuck"
  arity 2
}
gitlab_ci_pipeline_terminates(handle: gitlab_ci_pipeline_handle,
                              p: perturbation) -> verdict { \ }

# === gitlab_ci_invocation_well_formed composed bilateral ===
#
# Composes:
#   1. gitlab_ci_subcommand_admissible(subcommand, args, p)
#   2. tool_invocation_admissible(invocation, p) — inherited from
#      @tool family-root
#   3. gitlab_ci_config_valid(invocation, p) — WHEN subcommand
#      is `lint` (lint paths are config-validity-load-bearing)
#   4. gitlab_ci_pipeline_terminates(handle, p) — WHEN subcommand
#      is `pipeline_create` / `pipeline_status` (dispatch paths
#      are termination-load-bearing)
#
# The composed bilateral lifts the family-root discipline to the
# gitlab_ci species. Nth altitude of composed-bilateral pattern
# per @io/git.git_well_formed + @tool/cargo.cargo_invocation_
# well_formed + @tool/go.go_invocation_well_formed + @tool/docker.
# docker_invocation_well_formed (this session).
bilateral gitlab_ci_invocation_well_formed {
  sentinel "tool=gitlab-ci-subcommand-admissible-config-valid-pipeline-terminates"
  arity 4
}
gitlab_ci_invocation_well_formed(invocation: tool_invocation, p: perturbation)
  -> verdict
requires gitlab_ci_subcommand_admissible(invocation, invocation, p)
requires tool_invocation_admissible(invocation, invocation, p)
{ \ }

# ============================================================
# §5 Forward-promised
# ============================================================
#
# 1. `@io/http` mint at `shards/io/http.mirror` — the mechanism-
#    side @io species for HTTPS dispatch that the REST-fallback
#    multiplex composes over. Currently forward-promised.
# 2. `@io/git/forge/gitlab` mint at `shards/io/git/forge/gitlab.
#    mirror` — the ORTHOGONAL git-forge adapter for PR/issue/
#    release surfaces via git protocol (per @io/git §6 forward-
#    promise). Distinct altitude from THIS species.
# 3. `@code/gitlab_ci` mint at `shards/code/gitlab_ci.mirror` —
#    the form-side `.gitlab-ci.yml` declarative grammar. Would
#    compose with @code/yaml (LANDED at `shards/mirror/data/yaml.
#    mirror`) as species-refinement.
# 4. `@tool/glab` refinement — the glab CLI itself has its own
#    subcommand tree (glab issue / glab mr / glab api / etc.);
#    per-glab-subcommand refinements MAY need species-refinement
#    if the CI multiplex isn't the only glab usage.
# 5. Cross-forge equivalents — `@tool/github_actions` (GitHub
#    Actions workflows), `@tool/gitea_actions` (Gitea equivalent),
#    `@tool/circleci` (CircleCI CLI). Sibling species mints
#    forward-promised as consumers pin them.
# 6. Pipeline-graph reasoning — the `.gitlab-ci.yml` job graph IS a
#    substrate-decl'd DAG; `@spectral/mosaic.read(pipeline_config)`
#    forward-promised composition for CI-topology observation.
# 7. Secret-management composition — CI variables (especially
#    protected variables) compose with @io/secrets (LANDED at
#    `shards/io/secrets.mirror`); species-decl'd sibling forward-
#    promised.

out @tool/gitlab_ci
out gitlab_ci_subcommand
out gitlab_ci_pipeline_config
out gitlab_ci_pipeline_handle
out gitlab_ci_pipeline_status
out exec
out lint_config
out pipeline_create
out pipeline_status
out version_of
out gitlab_ci_subcommand_admissible
out gitlab_ci_config_valid
out gitlab_ci_pipeline_terminates
out gitlab_ci_invocation_well_formed
